// `dynamic X` asks a different question from every other type position — not *does this name
// exist* but *does it name an INTERFACE* — and stage-1 asked neither. Three acceptance divergences
// hid behind that: an unknown name in a parameter, the same in a field, and `dynamic` naming a
// class, which is the case this fixture pins.
//
// A CLASS rather than a missing name on purpose. A name that does not exist is refused for two
// reasons at once, so a fixture using one cannot say which rule caught it. `Point` exists and is
// still wrong here, so only the interface rule can produce this message.
//
// Stage-0's advice is the same for both — *declare it with `interface Point`* — and that is right
// rather than lazy: somebody who wrote `dynamic Point` over a class wanted an interface and does
// not have one.
class Point { x: Int, y: Int }

function describe(shape: dynamic Point) -> Int {
    return 1;
}

print(describe(Point { x: 1, y: 2 }));
